.TH "TDISK-LIST-PARTITIONS" "8" "2026-4-18" "TigerOS 0.2.1" "System Administration"

.SH NAME
tdisk-list-partitions \- list partitions on a disk or disk image.

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tdisk list-partitions

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBlist-partitions\fR command lists all partitions found on a disk. Partitions are subdivisions of disks that can be managed independently. Using this command, you can view the partitions that are on the disk.

.SH OUTPUT
The output will be formatted into a table with columns for:
.IP \\(bu
\fBPartition Number\fR - Used to identify the partition in the partition table. Ranged 1-4 in primary partitions.
.IP \\(bu
\fBType\fR - Whether the partition is a primary partition (stored in the main partition table) or an extended partition (stored in an extended partition table)
.IP \\(bu
\fBBoot\fR - Whether the partition is bootable/active. Bootable partitions usually have a volume boot record containing code to boot that partition.
.IP \\(bu
\fBSystem Type\fR - Descriptor of the partition's file system or purpose. (e.g. FAT32, Linux, NTFS)
.IP \\(bu
\fBFirst\fR - The first sector of the partition.
.IP \\(bu
\fBLast\fR - The last sector of the partition.
.IP \\(bu
\fBSize\fR - The total size of the partition.

.SH AUTHOR

\fBtdisk\fR was created by tigercodes-dev \fB<https://github.com/tigercodes-dev>\fR as part of the TigerOS project.

